# Line-ending policy.
#
# The repository stores every text file with LF in the object database, and the
# working tree is LF too - that is what cargo, rustfmt, vite and the CI runners
# write, and it is the only convention that keeps the checkout byte-identical to
# the index on every platform. Without this file the checkout follows the host's
# `core.autocrlf`, so a Windows clone gets CRLF working-tree files while
# `git diff` still reports them as unmodified; the first tool that rewrites one
# of those files (rustfmt, an editor) flips it to LF and the file shows up as
# fully modified. Pinning `eol=lf` removes that mismatch.
#
# `text=auto` makes Git normalise line endings on the way in, so a commit made
# from a CRLF working tree still stores LF. `eol=lf` then forces the checkout to
# LF as well.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows scripts. Command Prompt's `.bat` parser has known traps around LF-only
# line endings (a `goto` or a labelled block can be misread), so keep those as
# CRLF explicitly. `.ps1` is listed for the same reason: it is executed by
# Windows PowerShell on this project's only target platform.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Not text: never touched by end-of-line conversion. Listed explicitly (rather
# than relying on auto-detection) so a future binary format is not accidentally
# normalised.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.webp binary
*.ico binary
*.icns binary
*.gif binary
*.ttf binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.jar binary
*.dll binary
*.exe binary
*.so binary
*.dylib binary
*.bin binary

# SVG and NSIS sources are text; they are listed so auto-detection never has to
# guess.
*.svg text
*.nsh text
